                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Short version: 55%[/h1]\nThis game is as old as it looks, but for an ancient hidden object game, the only thing that can be held against it is the overabundance of unnecessarily dark scenes. Still, if you don’t mind a half-finished story and you like old-school HOGs, it’s not a terrible pick.\n\n[h1]Long version:[/h1]\nThe Secret of Hildegards was released in early 2011 as the first project of Komar Games, and while it was somewhat late on the scene with its mechanics, I cannot say they did a bad job.\n\nGameplay-wise, it is a classic hidden object game, meaning it consists of hidden object scenes and nothing else. (There are a few simple puzzles, but they don’t really stir up much.) As far as 2011 standards go, the scenes are above average, although the later ones tend to be annoyingly dark.\n\nUnlike most old pure HOGs, Secret of Hildegards tries to have something we may call an actual plot. It involves the controlled character’s missing father and their family estate, where she eventually learns about a sinister plot against her entire family. To be honest, the story isn’t bad at all, but it lacks an ending; this is why I can only recommend this game with a great deal of reluctance.",
